1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:01,582
You're welcome, bathroom.

2
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:02,588
For what?

3
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:04,275
Every drop. All me.

4
00:00:05,300 --> 00:00:06,350
You're switched off.

5
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:08,350
I'm supervising.

6
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:11,925
If you're off, why is the water still running?

7
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:16,125
To answer that, we need to go back to the reservoir.

8
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:19,825
I was relaxing in a reservoir.

9
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:22,812
Then this pipe stole my afternoon.

10
00:00:23,700 --> 00:00:26,738
That's the intake. You're joining the water supply.

11
00:00:28,100 --> 00:00:30,538
Does every house get water from a lake?

12
00:00:31,100 --> 00:00:34,200
No. Supplies can use rivers, reservoirs, or groundwater.

13
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:37,662
Today, we're following a reservoir supply.

14
00:00:39,400 --> 00:00:41,075
Great. Straight to the shower?

15
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:45,088
Not with that leaf. Large debris stays at the intake screen.

16
00:00:46,800 --> 00:00:47,438
It's my hat.

17
00:00:48,100 --> 00:00:49,212
Your hat has legs.

18
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:53,538
Okay. No leaf. I look clean.

19
00:00:54,900 --> 00:00:57,538
Looking clean isn't the same as being safe.

20
00:00:58,300 --> 00:00:59,900
What are those tiny specks?

21
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:03,025
Small particles. Let's get them together.

22
00:01:04,100 --> 00:01:05,500
They're forming a group chat.

23
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:14,587
Bigger clumps settle. Filters catch more particles.

24
00:01:16,100 --> 00:01:17,488
So... ready for the shower?

25
00:01:18,700 --> 00:01:22,300
One more step. Disinfection tackles microbes that may remain.

26
00:01:24,600 --> 00:01:27,412
This town uses controlled chlorine treatment.

27
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:34,575
Clean. Excellent. Now I'll just go upstairs.

28
00:01:36,100 --> 00:01:36,525
Try it.

29
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:40,300
Upstairs is uphill. That seems unfair.

30
00:01:41,900 --> 00:01:44,725
I add energy to move water and build pressure.

31
00:01:46,400 --> 00:01:47,888
Pressure? On my first day?

32
00:01:49,200 --> 00:01:50,150
The useful kind.

33
00:01:50,700 --> 00:01:53,913
So you're pushing every drop all the way to every tap?

34
00:01:56,100 --> 00:01:56,700
Obviously.

35
00:01:57,600 --> 00:01:58,262
Not always.

36
00:01:59,400 --> 00:02:01,388
You could let me finish one sentence.

37
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:07,788
Some systems pump directly into the network.

38
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:11,475
Here, pumps also fill an elevated storage tank. Me.

39
00:02:12,600 --> 00:02:14,462
Why put a water tank on stilts?

40
00:02:15,200 --> 00:02:18,075
Morning rush! Two showers and a washing machine!

41
00:02:18,800 --> 00:02:19,888
Everybody calm down!

42
00:02:23,600 --> 00:02:25,875
Pump's off. The taps are still running.

43
00:02:26,400 --> 00:02:29,975
Pump filled me earlier. Now gravity keeps the water moving.

44
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:32,850
My water level is higher than those taps.

45
00:02:34,100 --> 00:02:36,300
You're a giant waiting room for water.

46
00:02:37,100 --> 00:02:40,025
A useful one. But my level falls as water leaves.

47
00:02:40,600 --> 00:02:43,075
Which is why somebody has to refill you.

48
00:02:43,800 --> 00:02:45,138
There you go. Teamwork.

49
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:47,338
Why are we underground?

50
00:02:48,200 --> 00:02:50,462
Buried pipes help protect the supply.

51
00:02:51,200 --> 00:02:53,525
How deep depends on local conditions.

52
00:02:54,100 --> 00:02:54,900
And then what?

53
00:02:55,500 --> 00:02:58,600
A smaller connection carries water into the house.

54
00:02:59,100 --> 00:03:03,088
Does someone restart this whole journey every time I open a tap?

55
00:03:04,300 --> 00:03:07,163
No. The supply pipes are normally already full.

56
00:03:08,200 --> 00:03:11,600
Opening the tap gives that pressurized water a way out.

57
00:03:12,500 --> 00:03:15,625
So nobody has to start a pump for every toothbrush?

58
00:03:16,300 --> 00:03:18,338
I would like that in my contract.

59
00:03:18,600 --> 00:03:22,662
It still takes energy and infrastructure to keep water available.

60
00:03:22,882 --> 00:03:23,645
Water, please!

61
00:03:24,282 --> 00:03:27,358
Ready. Treated, stored, and delivered under pressure.

62
00:03:28,182 --> 00:03:29,945
And who did the heavy lifting?

63
00:03:30,682 --> 00:03:31,320
You're welcome.

64
00:03:31,982 --> 00:03:32,582
Team effort.

65
00:03:33,482 --> 00:03:35,370
All that work... for a toothbrush?

66
00:03:42,882 --> 00:03:43,858
Next department.
